h3. In place of introduction to my analysis

Looking at search / home screen, we have the following endpoints of interes (together with their admin counterparts):

*  {{"/internal/findProvidersInUserRange"}}
*  {{"/internal/findProvidersInUserRange/v2"}}

The responses are similar as we know, returning either a {{Provider}} or a {{ProviderV2}} object. Due to our current effort to unify all provider indexes, the same data (with some naming and grouping differences) is returned.

One issue is the fact that the same response is also used for 

* {{"/internal/findProvidersByIds"}}
* {{"/internal/findProvidersByIds/v2"}}

In this case we will need the entire data to be returned and we will probably go to ES instead of redis.

{color:#0747a6}However, a first problem is the fact that we use common interfaces, which might not work as well in the future{color}

h4. Checking what it is used for providers filtering

I will look first from the perspective of the data we require for filtering the providers of interest. These fields must be present in Redis, either in the same redis, or in a different one (to be discussed if it might make sense)

At the moment, for a provider, we keep the following data in redis:

{noformat}export interface ProviderData {
    id: number;
    name: TranslatableField;
    description: TranslatableField | null;
    promotion: TranslatableField | null;
    type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderType;
    status: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderStatus | null;
    city_id: number;
    vendor_id: number;
    coordinates: {lat: number; lng: number};
    tag_ids: number[];
    traits: ProviderDocumentTrait[];
    image: string;
    delivery_type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderActiveDeliveryType;
    supported_delivery_type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderSupportedDeliveryType;
    contact_details: ProviderContactDetailsV2;
    menu: ProviderDocumentMenuMetadata;
    in_courier_range: boolean | null;
    is_test: boolean | null;
    custom_delivery_radius_in_km: number | null;
    schedule: Schedule | null;
    currency: string | null;
    min_order_price: number | null;
    small_order_fee_cap: number | null;
    delivery_pricing: ProviderDocumentPricingPreferences;
    accept_cash_from: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.CashPaymentsAccepted | null;
    estimates: ProviderDocumentEstimates;
    hide_oot_dishes: boolean | null;
    allows_notes: ProviderDocumentNoteSettings;
    supports_pickup: boolean | null;
    exclusive_couriers: number[] | null;
    exclusive_eaters: number[] | null;
    delivery_polygon: string | null;
    extra_info?: ProviderExtraInfo[];
    popular_items_tagging_settings: PopularItemsTaggingSettings | null;
    price_level?: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.PriceLevel;
}{noformat}

On V2 flow, there are 2 strategies used that depends if we want to search providers or return the home screen providers. After looking at both strategies, the conclusion is that there is more filtering done when retrieving home screen providers than when searching based on provider’s name. Also, the fields used are quite similar:

* {{delivery_polygon}}
** used to compute {{includes_user_in_delivery_poly}} field 
** the computation happens in {{ProviderSearchService.updateProvidersWithPolygonInclusion()}}
** {color:#0747a6}Andrei mentioned that we might be able to improve how we store the polygons in redis and reduce space ??{color}
* before returning the provider selection for home screen, the {{ProviderFilterUtils.applyNonQueryStringFiltersOnDocuments()}} is called, and the following data is required in deciding what providers should be part of the final cut:
** {{status}}
** {{type}}
** {{exclusive_eaters}}
** {{custom_delivery_radius_in_km}}
** {{tag_ids}}
* we also support trait based filtering, so {{traits}} is another field required, especially given that we need access to the provider traits to filter out dine-in providers
* the final step is to compute availability for each provider; for that we require the following data:
** {{id}}
** {{exclusive_courier}}
** {{exclusive_eaters}}
** {{custom_delivery_radius_in_km}}
** {{coordinates.lat}}, {{coordinates.lng}}
** {{includes_user_in_delivery_poly}} which was computed based on provider’s polygon

At the end, we return to {{delivery-provider-search}} the provider data. As a result, for filtering and adjacent operations, we need the following fields:

{noformat}-> delivery_polygon
-> status
-> type
-> exclusive_eaters
-> exclusive_couriers
-> custom_delivery_radius_in_km
-> tag_ids
-> traits
-> provider_id
-> coordinates{noformat}



h3. Looking for useless data in Redis:

Before going further with my analysis, I wanted to answer the question: Is there anything in redis that it is not used for filtering, nor returned as part of the endpoints response ? My analysis showed that currently there are 2 fields that are not used anywhere when data is retrieved from redis:

* {{city_id}} → which comes with the request as well
* {{in_courier_range}} → which is an artifact and given that we now take data from marketplace, we should remove it.


There is one more thing to consider here, prior to returning to {{delivery-provider-search}} we also send unused providers to analytics, so we will need to keep  in redis as well the data that we send to analytics: 

{noformat}    export interface UnusedHomeScreenProvider {
        provider_id: number;
        vendor_id: number;
        tags: string;
        trait_slugs: string[];
        has_promotion: boolean;
        is_delivery_available: boolean;
        is_pickup_available: boolean;
        unavailability_reasons?: string;
        distance_in_km?: number;
        max_small_order_fee?: number;
        min_order_price?: number;
        currency: string;
        location: LatLng;
        pricing_preferences: DeliveryPricingEaterInternalAsyncApi.ProviderPricingPreference;
        delivery_type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderActiveDeliveryType;
        estimates: DeliveryEaterDataStoreInternalAsyncApi.Estimates;
        supported_delivery_type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderSupportedDeliveryType;
    }
}{noformat}



I will focus now on the data that is returned from {{delivery-eater-data-store}} and what it is actually used in upstream services. Once we have a list of fields that are not used for the moment, we can cross reference them with what we send to analytics and check the overlap.

h3. Converting between V2 responses and V1 in {{delivery-provider-search}}

First, we return the following structure:

{noformat}export interface ProviderV2 {
    id: number;
    name: LocalisedString;
    description?: LocalisedString;
    promotion?: LocalisedString;
    type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderType;
    status: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderStatus;

    vendor_id: number;
    location: Location;

    tag_ids: number[];
    trait_slugs: string[];

    image: string;

    delivery_type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderActiveDeliveryType;
    supported_delivery_type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderSupportedDeliveryType;
    contact_details: ProviderContactDetailsV2;

    menu: ProviderMenuMetadata;

    is_test?: boolean;
    distance_to_eater_km?: number;
    custom_delivery_radius_in_km?: number;

    schedule?: ProviderScheduleV2;

    currency?: string;
    min_order_price?: number;
    small_order_fee_cap?: number;
    delivery_pricing?: DeliveryPricingPreferences;
    accept_cash_from?: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.CashPaymentsAccepted;

    estimates: Estimates;

    hide_oot_dishes?: boolean;

    note_settings: NoteSettings;
    supports_pickup?: boolean;
    // TODO: make this non nullable when it is safe
    availability?: PerFlowProviderAvailability;

    extra_info?: ProviderExtraInfoLabelToValue[];
    popular_items_tagging_settings?: PopularItemsTaggingSettings;
    price_level?: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.PriceLevel;
}{noformat}

On {{delivery-provider-service}}, the data received from V2 endpoints is converted to V1 format in {{ProviderDataService.buildProviderFromV2()}}.  Looking closely at this conversion, the following fields do not have an equivalent in the V1 format, yet they are stored in Redis and returned:

{noformat}export interface ProviderV2 {
        menu: {
            id?: number;
            dish_count?: number;
            variant_ids?: number[];
        },
        distance_to_eater_km?: number;
        popular_items_tagging_settings?: PopularItemsTaggingSettings;
}{noformat}

Going forward with the analysis, we will look at what data from V1 format is actually used and what fields can be removed safely. 

h3. The problem with {{/internal/findProvidersByIds/v2/}}

However, a problem here is that discovery is also using a single data model to handle both provider data for home screen and search, and also provider data when the actual provider id is known. As a result any initiative to remove data will require either operating with {{null}} values or splitting interfaces.

I also want to point out that the {{ProviderV1}} interface have all the fields from above. It’s just they are returned only via V1 flows. This raise the question, why do we have a {{"/internal/findProvidersByIds/v2"}} endpoint in the first place ? I think it’s adding some confusion and we should think of having a single {{findProvidersByIds}} endpoint.

So, based on my analysis, there are currently 2 endpoints that use the v2 version of {{"/internal/findProvidersByIds"}}:

* {{"/deliveryClient/getNearestProviderDetails"}} → which searches for a single provider and it is used for bolt market banner
* {{"/deliveryClient/getProviderDetails"}} → which as well searches for a single provider

What’s more interesting is that there is significant difference in the response time for the 2 versions of the same endpoint, the old one being faster while handling more traffic.

!Screenshot 2024-08-21 at 17.59.45.png|width=1728,height=566,alt="Screenshot 2024-08-21 at 17.59.45.png"!

h3. What we return but it’s not used



Based on my analysis of {{delivery-provider-search}}, for {{/internal/findProvidersInUserRange}} we have the following data that is returned, converted from V2 to V1, but it’s not used further down the line:

{noformat}interface Provider {
    status: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderStatus;
    schedule: ProviderSchedule | null; -> delivery only, no takeway
    currency: string | null; --> the question is if we can use the city settings instead of per provider data ??
    is_test: boolean;
    hide_out_of_serving_interval_dishes: boolean | null;
    note_settings: ProviderNoteSettings;
    supports_order_pickup: boolean | null;
    menu_items_count?: number;
    extra_info?: ProviderExtraInfoLabelToValue[];
    popular_items_tagging_settings?: PopularItemsTaggingSettings;
    menu_id?: number;
    menu_variant_ids?: number[];
}{noformat}

