Based on my analysis, part of which can be consulted in a coment below, I have a few conclusions that I want to make:

* Currently, the following data is stored in redis:

{noformat}export interface ProviderData {
    id: number;
    name: TranslatableField;
    description: TranslatableField | null;
    promotion: TranslatableField | null;
    type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderType;
    status: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderStatus | null;
    city_id: number;
    vendor_id: number;
    coordinates: {lat: number; lng: number};
    tag_ids: number[];
    traits: ProviderDocumentTrait[];
    image: string;
    delivery_type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderActiveDeliveryType;
    supported_delivery_type: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.ProviderSupportedDeliveryType;
    contact_details: ProviderContactDetailsV2;
    menu: ProviderDocumentMenuMetadata;
    in_courier_range: boolean | null;
    is_test: boolean | null;
    custom_delivery_radius_in_km: number | null;
    schedule: Schedule | null;
    currency: string | null;
    min_order_price: number | null;
    small_order_fee_cap: number | null;
    delivery_pricing: ProviderDocumentPricingPreferences;
    accept_cash_from: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.CashPaymentsAccepted | null;
    estimates: ProviderDocumentEstimates;
    hide_oot_dishes: boolean | null;
    allows_notes: ProviderDocumentNoteSettings;
    supports_pickup: boolean | null;
    exclusive_couriers: number[] | null;
    exclusive_eaters: number[] | null;
    delivery_polygon: string | null;
    extra_info?: ProviderExtraInfo[];
    popular_items_tagging_settings: PopularItemsTaggingSettings | null;
    price_level?: DeliveryProviderInternalAsyncApi.PriceLevel;
}{noformat}

* When removing everything that is needed for:
** filtering
** sending to analytics
** computing availability
** required by {{delivery-provider-search}} for additional operations
** sent from {{delivery-provider-search}} to upstream clients
* We get the following fields that are not necessary to be stored in redis if redis will be used exclusively for calls to {{findProvidersInUserRange}} (home screen + name search)

{noformat}Can Be Removed From Redis Provider Data {
    city_id: number; -> can be retrieved from request, not needed per provider
    menu: ProviderDocumentMenuMetadata {
      id?: number | null;
      dish_count?: number | null;
      item_count?: number | null;
      variant_ids?: number[] | null;
    } -> not needed for search
    in_courier_range: boolean | null; -> artifact that has no value anymore
    is_test: boolean | null; -> not used anyhere from what I've managed to dig up
    schedule: Schedule | null; {
      pickup: DailySchedule | null;
      delivery: DailySchedule | null; -> only this is used
    },
    currency: string | null; -> can we take it from city settings ???
    hide_oot_dishes: boolean | null; -> not used
    allows_notes: ProviderDocumentNoteSettings; -> not used
    supports_pickup: boolean | null; -> not used
    extra_info?: ProviderExtraInfo[]; -> not used
    popular_items_tagging_settings: PopularItemsTaggingSettings | null; -> not used
}
{noformat}

* I would argue that there will not be a significant reduction in data stored if we eliminate all these fields from redis; by significant I refer to 50% or more. Still, fields like {{extra_info}}, {{schedule.pickup}}, {{menu}} and {{popular_items_tagging_settins}} contains a decent amount of data such that some increase in performance might be noticed if we decide to make the Redis leaner. If we could optimise some additional fields (like delivery_polygons), or combine this approach with a compression, I think the improvements might be more visible. 
* These being said, some points I want to mention:
** Currently we are using the same interfaces for both {{findByIds}} and {{findProvidersInUserRange}}.  I think we should discuss separating the flows and keeping a reduced interface for {{findProvidersInUserRange}} if we want to continue with this approach.
** The same is valid for {{delivery-provider-search}}. There the flows and data structures should also be separated, as the code is quite messy and not that easy to follow. 
** Another point worth mentioning is that we currently have 2 endpoints for {{findByIds}} flow:
*** {{"/internal/findProvidersByIds"}}
*** {{"/internal/findProvidersByIds/v2"}}
*** In {{delivery-provider-search}} there is a conversion between V2 interfaces and V1. However the data is not 1:1 and as I mentioned in the analysis, the v2 endpoint seems to be slower than the V1. 
*** I still support the idea that Redis should be used only for home screen and search flows, and in such case is there any value in having 2 different endpoints for retrieving providers when we know the ids ?
** I also tried to look a bit further upstream, to {{delivery-eater-screen}} service. While things got even harder to follow, I noticed that there, as well, are field that are retrieved from {{delivery-provider-search}} that are not used (but we would need to involve discovery team for a more thorough analysis and if we need to do changes there as well)
{noformat}export interface InternalProviderSearchResult {
        retrieval_score?: number | null;
        relevance_ranking_score?: number;
        response_ranking_version?: string;
        description: LocalisedString | null;
        schedule: ScheduleV0 | null;
        price_level_str: string;
        rating: number | null;
        provider_snackbar_text: LocalisedString | null;
        is_available: boolean;  
        phone: string;
        show_conditional_label: boolean;
        dispatch_type: DeliveryDispatchType;
}{noformat}

