Hi [~accountid:63e3ef2dbf837c6893db58e3] - 

* I have considered your request and analysed the applicable legal framework. I have included a detailed analysis  below.
* In short, while I appreciate the operational thinking behind your proposal, *it would not be legally permitted* under the Personal Data Protection Act (PDPA) in Thailand:
** We will require *explicit consent* from Drivers to process criminal record data. 
** It *cannot be bundled* into the Ts&Cs. 
** We will also need to *explain the consequences of not providing consent*, or withdrawing consent. I can help review the consent that Drivers need to click “I agree” on and the wording around the consequences of not providing it. 
* From a practical point of view, I highly suggest that the consent is included in the Driver Registration Flow and _not as an afterthought_ as then you will likely continue to see lower numbers of Drivers providing their consent. By making the consent a requirement to proceed with registration, you can ensure that anyone who wants to Drive with Bolt will need to consent. We would obviously explain the consequences of not providing consent, which would include an ability to complete their registration. 

----

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*General Principles in relation to obtaining consent in Thailand*
{panel}

The Personal Data Protection Committee (PDPC) in Thailand has made it very clear in published Guidelines that: 

* Consent must be obtained prior to or at the time of processing of personal data;
* Data subjects must be informed on purpose(s) and detail(s) of obtaining the consent prior to obtaining consent from the data subject;
* Purpose(s) of the consent must be specific;
* {color:#4c9aff}*Consent request must be separated from other texts/documents*{color};
* Wording used in the request must be unambiguous and use plain language and not be deceptive or misleading to the data subject;
* Consent from the data subject must be freely given; and
* {color:#4c9aff}*Consent must not be used as a precondition for entering into a contract or for the provision of any services*{color} if such processing of personal data is not required or is not related to the contract or the provision of services in question.

From the highlighted text, it is clear that the PDPC has aligned itself with EU standards around ‘adequate consent' and we therefore +cannot+ bundle consent into the Driver Ts&Cs.


{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Explicit Consent for Processing Sensitive Personal Data (including criminal records)*
{panel}

* Section 26 of the PDPA prohibits collection of “sensitive person data” unless the data controller obtains *explicit consent* from the data subject or an exception applies. 
* In this case, there are +no applicable exceptions+. Sensitive personal data includes criminal records.



{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*PDPC Notification on the collection and processing of personal data related to criminal records*
{panel}

* Under the draft notification, data controllers may process criminal record data for the purpose of a recruitment process, *checking the qualifications of personnel, and considering the suitability of a person for a position if the processing activities are required by law or when a data controller obtains explicit consent from the data subject*. Furthermore, the necessity of processing the criminal record data must be announced at the beginning of the recruitment process (see Article 5 of the [criteria for personal data protection measures for collecting personal data |https://files.law.go.th/dgaBackoffice/2023-11-14-09%3A35%3A33_%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%AF%20%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%8D%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%20%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%2026%20%E0%B8%89%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%9F%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AB%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%99.pdf]published by the PDPC).
* Data controllers’ requests for explicit consent to collect a data subject’s criminal record data must also notify the data subject of the consequences of not providing consent or withdrawing consent (see Article 6 of the [criteria for personal data protection measures for collecting personal data |https://files.law.go.th/dgaBackoffice/2023-11-14-09%3A35%3A33_%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%AF%20%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%8D%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%20%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%2026%20%E0%B8%89%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%9F%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AB%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%99.pdf]published by the PDPC).

