Short summary of this feature.

Users can cancel orders without CS involvement. We implemented a set of rules which makes the order cancelable by user. This is done by a cancel order button shown on active order. Once the rule(s) don’t apply anymore the order button is not shown anymore. 

The feature is under a feature flag which describe what rule is active and extra parameters for configuration of each rule. Example of FF json 

{noformat}{
    "delivery_order_enable_cancellation_button": true,
    "provider_did_not_accept_the_order_config": {
        "is_enabled": true
    },
    "scheduled_order_before_starting_preparation_config": {
        "is_enabled": true
    },
    "preparation_time_took_too_long_config": {
        "is_enabled": true,
        "preparation_time_max_delay_seconds": 120
    },
    "courier_did_not_arrive_at_the_provider_in_time_config": {
        "is_enabled": true,
        "courier_arriving_at_provider_max_delay_seconds": 120
    },
    "courier_did_not_arrive_at_eater_in_time_config": {
        "is_enabled": true,
        "courier_arriving_at_eater_max_delay_seconds": 120
    }
}{noformat}



[https://admin-panel.prelive.bolt.eu/experiment-targeting/targeting/featureflags/edit/9795|https://admin-panel.prelive.bolt.eu/experiment-targeting/targeting/featureflags/edit/9795]



The rules are the following:

# A order can be cancelled before it is accepted by the provider.
# If it is a scheduled order, and the provider didn’t start the preparation then the order can be cancelled.
# If the provider takes too much time to prepare the order (too much time is defined as a time greater than the estimated cooking time + X min (X being a constant defined on the city level).  The estimated cooking time is the one set by each provider in the admin panel (average cooking time). 
# If order delivery takes too much time (this means that the time between the provider marking the order as ready and the courier arriving at the restaurant is bigger than Y min (Y is a constant defined at the city level). Once the courier arrives at the provider the order is not cancelable anymore.
# The courier took too much time to deliver the order (the time difference between picking up the order and arriving at the client shouldn’t take more than Z minutes.



Happy flow case:

User is on active order → a rule is triggered → cancel button is shown → user presses the button → an additional pop-up will show and ask the user if he/she is sure to cancel the order → user presses yes → order is cancelled and a new screen is showing asking for reasons → user can choose a reason (or not) of why cancelling the order

Important things to mention:

* once the user presses that he/she is sure the order is *canceled*
* we don’t charge the user for any of the rules (no money is taken from his card)
*  except rule 2, if any actor did any amount of work then we compensate the actor. Eg. on rule 3 cooking time takes too much → User cancels  → we compensate the provider even if it is their fault. On rule 1,2 nobody gets paid (no actor did any of work → no one is compensated). On rule 5, courier takes too much to deliver the order → user cancels → we still compensate the courier
* the cancel order button show logic is based on a cron which runs every minute (so expect that we don’t show/hide the button instantly when the rule is enabled/disabled). Anyway, even if we show the button and the rule is not applied anymore if the user cancels it (presses the button fast) we do an additional check on BE and don’t let the user cancel the order)



Let me know if you have additional questions.





